WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.670
Good morning, everyone!

2
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.116
Welcome to our interview.

3
00:00:10.116 --> 00:00:13.124
Today, we are going to
discover the Wayuu community,

4
00:00:13.124 --> 00:00:16.190
one of Colombia's most
important Indigenous cultures.

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.374
The Wayuu people live mainly in La
Guajira, Colombia, and Venezuela.

6
00:00:20.374 --> 00:00:24.390
They are known for their traditions,
colorful clothing, and unique language.

7
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.106
Today, we will explore their culture,

8
00:00:28.106 --> 00:00:31.553
communication system, traditions,
and language with our special guests,

9
00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:32.622
Isabella and Santiago.

10
00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:33.010
Welcome!

11
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.915
Thank you for inviting us!

12
00:00:34.915 --> 00:00:36.760
We are happy to share
our culture with you.

13
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:37.901
Hello, everyone!

14
00:00:37.901 --> 00:00:40.060
We hope you enjoy learning
about the Wayuu people.

15
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.020
Let's begin by learning
about your way of life.

16
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.690
What makes Wayuu culture special?

17
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.516
Family, respect, and community
are very important to us.

18
00:00:54.516 --> 00:00:56.840
Our society is organized into clans,

19
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.778
and family identity is passed
down through the mother's side.

20
00:01:00.778 --> 00:01:04.200
Our elders teach us about
our history and traditions.

21
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.360
We also have a strong
connection to our territory.

22
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.126
Some Wayuu families raise goats,
fish, and make traditional crafts.

23
00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:15.510
Our colorful clothing and handmade bags
are important symbols of our identity.

24
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.470
Communication is an important
part of every culture.

25
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.930
How do Wayuu people communicate
and solve conflicts?

26
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.042
We traditionally use stories, songs,
and conversations to share knowledge.

27
00:01:31.042 --> 00:01:35.450
Parents and grandparents teach younger
generations through oral communication.

28
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:41.144
We also have the Palabrero, a traditional
mediator who helps families solve

29
00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:44.342
problems peacefully through
dialogue and agreements.

30
00:01:44.342 --> 00:01:47.910
Today, many Wayuu people also
use phones and social media.

31
00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.380
We have heard about your
beautiful crafts and celebrations.

32
00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.610
What are the most
important Wayuu traditions?

33
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.383
Weaving is one of our
most famous traditions.

34
00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:03.647
Many women make colorful bags called
mochilas and traditional hammocks.

35
00:02:03.647 --> 00:02:07.190
These skills are passed down
from generation to generation.

36
00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.542
Another tradition is the Yonna,
a dance performed during celebrations.

37
00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:14.708
Traditional music, storytelling,
and foods like goat meat,

38
00:02:14.708 --> 00:02:17.110
corn, and fish are also
part of our culture.

39
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.630
Language is also an important
part of cultural identity.

40
00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:26.500
What language do Wayuu people
speak, and why is it important?

41
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.639
Our traditional language
is called Wayuunaiki.

42
00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:33.050
It has its own grammar and vocabulary.

43
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.350
Many Wayuu people speak
both Wayuunaiki and Spanish.

44
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.350
Our language helps us preserve our
stories, traditions, and identity.

45
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.824
For example, Anasü means "good" or
"fine," depending on the context.

46
00:02:45.824 --> 00:02:49.640
Teaching Wayuunaiki to children
helps protect our culture.

47
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.550
Protecting Indigenous cultures is very
important, especially in the modern world.

48
00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.080
How do Wayuu people preserve
their culture today?

49
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.358
We preserve our culture by
teaching children our language,

50
00:03:04.358 --> 00:03:07.072
weaving, music, and traditions.

51
00:03:07.072 --> 00:03:11.450
Families and schools help
keep our heritage alive.

52
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.695
We also protect our culture through
celebrations and community activities.

53
00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:19.287
Other people can help by respecting
our traditions and supporting authentic

54
00:03:19.287 --> 00:03:19.910
Wayuu crafts.

55
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.351
Today, we learned about the Wayuu culture,

56
00:03:23.351 --> 00:03:25.780
traditions, communication
system, and language.

57
00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.150
We discovered how important family,

58
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.260
respect, and cultural identity
are to the Wayuu community.

59
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.990
This interview reminds us why we should
respect and protect Indigenous cultures.

60
00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.360
Thank you for inviting us!

61
00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:44.020
We hope you enjoyed
learning about our culture!

62
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.680
Goodbye!
